  ans: 1,
  exp: "An elevated screening blood pressure should be followed by standardized repeat measurements and appropriate clinical assessment to establish the diagnosis and guide treatment. Hypertension is frequently asymptomatic. Urgent emergency management is reserved for situations such as markedly elevated blood pressure accompanied by acute target-organ injury, not an isolated asymptomatic screening reading.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Hypertensive emergency):</strong> An asymptomatic elevated screening reading without acute target-organ injury does not by itself establish a hypertensive emergency.<br>• <strong>Option C (Hypertension excluded by absence of symptoms):</strong> Hypertension is often asymptomatic and cannot be excluded on that basis.<br>• <strong>Option D (Thrombolytic therapy):</strong> Thrombolysis is used for selected acute thrombotic conditions and has no role in routine hypertension screening."
