  q: "A 2-year-old child has bilateral pitting edema, sparse discolored hair, and skin changes. The child has a history of inadequate dietary intake. Which form of severe acute malnutrition is most strongly suggested by these findings?",
  opts: ["Marasmus without edema", "Kwashiorkor", "Isolated vitamin C deficiency", "Simple iron-deficiency anemia"],
  ans: 1,
  exp: "Kwashiorkor classically presents with bilateral pitting edema in the setting of severe malnutrition and may include skin changes, hair depigmentation, and apathy. Edema is an important distinguishing feature from marasmus, although severe acute malnutrition can have overlapping presentations and requires prompt clinical assessment.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (Marasmus without edema):</strong> Marasmus classically causes severe wasting without nutritional edema; bilateral pitting edema suggests edematous malnutrition.<br>• <strong>Option C (Vitamin C deficiency):</strong> Scurvy typically causes findings such as gingival changes, bone pain, and perifollicular hemorrhage rather than the characteristic edematous malnutrition syndrome.<br>• <strong>Option D (Iron-deficiency anemia):</strong> This causes anemia-related findings such as pallor and fatigue but does not explain bilateral pitting edema with characteristic skin and hair changes."
