  exp: "The systematic sampling interval is the population size divided by the desired sample size: k = N/n = 5,000/500 = 10. After choosing a random start from the first interval, every tenth household is selected. A periodic pattern in the list can introduce bias if it aligns with the sampling interval.",
  wrongExp: "• <strong>Option A (5):</strong> An interval of 5 would produce approximately 1,000 households from a list of 5,000.<br>• <strong>Option C (50):</strong> An interval of 50 would produce approximately 100 households, not 500.<br>• <strong>Option D (100):</strong> An interval of 100 would produce approximately 50 households."
